# Deploy — v0.1.11

Ships the Astro + Sanity rebuild of [www.tournewzealand.com](http://www.tournewzealand.com) as Cloudflare Worker `site-navi` — the first deploy from this repo, with forms rewired from Basin to Sygnal Studio; no migrations, and the Sanity dataset is untouched (backup is precautionary). `routes` is deliberately empty in `wrangler.prod.jsonc`, so this lands on the workers.dev URL only and the live Webflow domain is unaffected.

## Deploy

- [x] **1.** Export the Sanity production dataset. Precautionary — this release does not write to it. Lands at `migration/backup/production.tar.gz`.

```bash
npm --prefix studio run export
```

- [x] **2.** Deploy the Worker. The script guards that the dataset is `production`, builds `web/`, and deploys with `wrangler.prod.jsonc`.

```bash
npm run deploy
```

-   **3.** Cut-over is NOT part of this deploy: both `routes` entries in `wrangler.prod.jsonc` stay commented out, so the public site remains on Webflow until a future release attaches the custom domain.

- [ ] **4.** Critical check: request the URL `wrangler deploy` printed. Expect HTTP `200` — anything else, stop and roll back.

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" <deployed-workers-dev-url>
```

- [x] **5.** Tag the release.

```bash
git tag -a v0.1.11 -m "Release 0.1.11 — Astro + Sanity rebuild, first Worker deploy; forms on Sygnal Studio"
```

- [x] **6.** Push the tag.

```bash
git push origin v0.1.11
```

## If it goes wrong

Fully reversible by code alone: no migration shipped and the Sanity dataset was never written, so the backup from step 1 should not be needed. The live domain is not routed to this Worker, so the public site cannot be affected either way.

- [ ] **1.** Roll the Worker back to the previously deployed version.

```bash
npx wrangler rollback --config wrangler.prod.jsonc
```
